% Chapter 95 of the tariff (Section XX): toys, games and sports equipment;
% parts and accessories thereof — all six headings (9503 to 9508) and their
% 39 six-digit subheadings. A library: it includes chapter_42.le (and
% through it chapter_64.le, chapter_71.le, chapter_39.le, section_xi.le and
% gri.le), chapter_61.le, chapter_62.le and chapter_67.le, because those
% chapters send the articles of Chapter 95 out of their headings (Chapter
% 39 note 2(y), Chapter 42 note 2(l), Section XI note 1(t), Chapter 67
% note 1(e), Chapter 71 note 3(n)), and because note 1 of this chapter
% sends sports bags, apparel, footwear and gloves back to them. tariff.le
% includes it.
%
% Text: HTSUS Revision 23 (2026), Chapter 95 notes 1 to 6, subheading note
% 1, and the terms of the headings and subheadings (https://hts.usitc.gov/);
% Section XX has no notes. The subheadings are the six-digit HS ones. The
% EU Combined Nomenclature Explanatory Notes (CN EN) to Chapter 95 say what
% a stuffed toy, a building block, a toy drone, a festive article and a
% playing card are. US Customs rulings name the kinds of goods and show
% how the notes are read.
%
% How the chapter is read. The facts of a good are its kind (a doll, a
% puzzle, a yoga mat, a fishing reel...), and the features the notes and
% the subheadings name:
%   G is a part or accessory suitable for use solely or principally with K
%       (note 3: classified with the article of kind K)
%   G is marketed as a decoration for F   (heading 9505: a decoration made
%       for a festivity F, such as Christmas, Halloween or a birthday)
%   G is inflatable                       (subheading 9506.62)
%   G is operated by coins or other means of payment    (subheading 9504.30)
%   G has ice or roller skates attached   (subheading 9506.70; the template
%       of chapter_64.le)
% and the facts that take a good out of the chapter (note 1): it has a
% utilitarian function, it is a part of general use, it is identifiable as
% intended exclusively for animals (note 5), it is not principally designed
% for amusement (a finding of the office), its kind is one that note 1
% names. Each kind falls in a family: toy (9503), game (9504), festive
% article (9505), sports equipment (9506), fishing tackle (9507),
% fairground amusement (9508). Heading 9506 is residual: it covers only the
% articles "not specified or included elsewhere in this chapter".
%
% Not modelled: the lines below six digits (the age bands of heading 9503,
% the values of fishing reels); note 4 is read only through a judgment of
% the office (the combination has the essential character of toys); note 6
% (amusement park rides) is read through the kinds only; the goods of other
% chapters that note 1 names (candles, fireworks, bicycles, drones that are
% aircraft, radio remote controls, lighting strings...) are outside the
% model: such a good is reported, not classified.
%
% Provenance: every rule and table is labelled and cites the passage it
% encodes; the documents say where their text is (sources/), so the editor
% shows each passage and the verifier checks every quotation.
%
% DISCLAIMER. This program is an example of Logical English. It is provided
% "as is", without warranty of any kind, express or implied, including any
% warranty that it is accurate, complete, up to date or fit for a particular
% purpose. It is not an official reading of the tariff or of the rulings it
% cites, it is not a binding classification ruling, and it is not legal or
% customs advice. Its answers may be wrong: do not rely on them for a real
% import, export or declaration; consult the official texts, the customs
% authority and a qualified professional. Its authors accept no liability for
% any loss or damage arising from its use.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    *a family* is a family of chapter ninety-five goods.
    *a kind* is a kind of the subheading class *a class*.
    *a kind* is a kind of decoration.
    *a kind* is a kind named by note 1 to chapter ninety-five.
    *a good* is a part or accessory suitable for use solely or principally with *a kind*; undefined.
    *a good* is marketed as a decoration for *a festivity*; undefined.
    *a good* is inflatable; undefined.
    *a good* is operated by coins or other means of payment; undefined.
    *a good* has a utilitarian function; undefined.
    *a good* is a part of general use; undefined.
    *a good* is identifiable as intended exclusively for animals; undefined.
    *a good* is not principally designed for amusement; undefined.
    *a thing* has pearls or stones only as minor constituents; undefined.
    *a good* is put up for retail sale; undefined.
    *a good* is a combination put up for retail sale with items of other headings; undefined.
    *a good* has the essential character of toys; judged.
    *a good* is barred from heading *a heading*.
    *a good* is a good of heading *a heading* of chapter ninety-five.
    the subheading class of *a thing* is *a class*.
    the inflation of *a thing* is *a state*.
    the payment of *a thing* is *a means*.
    the festivity of *a thing* is *a festivity*.
    the chapter ninety-five code of *a thing* in heading *a heading* is *a code*.
    the subheading in heading *a heading* for the class *a class* with inflation *a state* with payment *a means* with festivity *a festivity* is *a code* under table toys and sports.
    the numerical order of *a code* is *a number* under table subheading order.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% GRI 6: the six-digit subheadings of headings 9503 to 9508. First match: a
% row with "any" in the class is the heading's "Other". The columns are the
% class of the article (from its kind, below), whether it is inflatable,
% whether it is operated by coins or other means of payment, and the
% festivity it is marketed for.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
the table toys and sports is, with first match, with provenance HTSUS General Rules of Interpretation,
